# 6. Case D — Cedar River / Landsburg Dam

### Starting condition

The Landsburg Dam historically restricted anadromous fish access to portions of the Cedar River.

A fish-passage facility was completed in 2003.

NOAA subsequently conducted long-term monitoring of salmon populations using the newly accessible habitat.

### Intervention

The principal intervention was:

**Barrier removal → habitat reconnection → long-term monitoring**

The case differs from Elwha because the intervention was more targeted rather than a watershed-scale dam-removal project.

### Evidence matrix

| Factor                       | Evidence                          |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Early intervention           | **Partially documented**          |
| Strong baseline data         | **Documented**                    |
| Explicit biological targets  | **Partially documented**          |
| Harvest reduction            | **Partially documented**          |
| Habitat protection           | **Documented**                    |
| Habitat restoration          | **Documented**                    |
| Monitoring                   | **Documented**                    |
| Long-term funding            | **Partially documented**          |
| Federal-state coordination   | **Documented**                    |
| Tribal participation         | **Partially documented**          |
| International coordination   | **Not applicable**                |
| Private-sector participation | **Partially documented**          |
| Adaptive management          | **Documented**                    |
| Enforcement/compliance       | **Not central**                   |
| Institutional continuity     | **Documented**                    |
| Biological improvement       | **Documented**                    |
| Durable improvement          | **Requires continued monitoring** |

### Institutional lesson

The Cedar River case is useful because it demonstrates the value of **longitudinal ecological measurement**.

The relevant conservation question was not simply whether fish passed the barrier.

It was whether fish populations subsequently established sustained use of the newly accessible habitat.

That distinction mirrors the project's broader performance framework:

**Infrastructure completed ≠ ecological outcome achieved.**

