# 7. Case E — Alameda Creek

### Starting condition

Alameda Creek contained barriers that restricted access by migratory fish, including threatened Central California Coast steelhead.

In 2025, NOAA reported completion of a project removing the final barrier to fish passage on a portion of the system. The project restored access to upstream spawning and juvenile-rearing habitat.

### Intervention

The principal intervention was targeted barrier removal.

Its importance lies in ecological connectivity.

A relatively localized infrastructure change can restore access to a much larger network of habitat.

### Evidence matrix

| Factor                       | Evidence                                     |
| ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Early intervention           | **Partially documented**                     |
| Strong baseline data         | **Documented**                               |
| Explicit biological targets  | **Partially documented**                     |
| Harvest reduction            | **Not principal intervention**               |
| Habitat protection           | **Documented**                               |
| Habitat restoration          | **Documented**                               |
| Monitoring                   | **Documented**                               |
| Long-term funding            | **Partially documented**                     |
| Federal-state coordination   | **Documented**                               |
| Tribal participation         | **Insufficient evidence for current matrix** |
| International coordination   | **Not applicable**                           |
| Private-sector participation | **Documented/Partially documented**          |
| Adaptive management          | **Partially documented**                     |
| Enforcement/compliance       | **Not central**                              |
| Institutional continuity     | **Requires further evidence**                |
| Biological improvement       | **Too early for final determination**        |
| Durable improvement          | **Insufficient evidence**                    |

### Institutional lesson

Alameda Creek illustrates the concept of the **ecological bottleneck**.

Restoration investments can remain underperforming when a single remaining barrier prevents organisms from reaching otherwise suitable habitat.

This suggests that conservation planning should identify not merely where degradation exists, but **which constraint prevents the ecological system from functioning**.

